  became foundational to mathematics and commerce throughout the ancient and medieval worlds.

  The exact origins of the abacus remain somewhat obscured by the mists of time, though most scholars believe it emerged
  independently in multiple ancient civilizations. The earliest evidence suggests that simple counting boards—flat
  surfaces marked with lines and used with pebbles or counters—appeared in Mesopotamia and Egypt around 2700 BCE. The
  Sumerian and Babylonian civilizations utilized such devices for accounting and astronomical calculations. However,
  these primitive counting boards bear only a conceptual resemblance to the abacus we recognize today, as they lacked
  the defining mechanical feature of beads strung on rods.

  The invention of the true abacus—with beads threaded on rods or strings—likely occurred in the eastern Mediterranean
  region, though the Phoenicians and Greeks deserve credit for early refinement. The Romans inherited and modified the
  Greek design, creating the [3mabacus[0m (or [3mabacu[0m in Latin), which became an essential tool for their empire's vast
  commercial and administrative networks. Roman soldiers carried portable abaci made of bronze or bone, using them for
  quick calculations during military campaigns and trade expeditions. The Roman abacus typically featured beads arranged
  in columns, with each column representing different values—ones, tens, hundreds, and so forth—allowing for efficient
  addition, subtraction, multiplication, and even division.

